<title>UK: Removal of EU aluminium duty will come from within.</title>
<headline>Removal of EU aluminium duty will come from within.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1997-06-23</dateline>
<text>
<p>The removal of the six percent tariff on primary aluminium within the European Union (EU) will come about through internal demands, Dan Horovitz, a partner at law-firm Theodore Goddard said.</p>
<p>&quot;Regarding the removal of the six percent duty the pressures need to come from within the EU to outweigh the need for protection,&quot; Horovitz said at the Financial Times World Aluminium Conference.</p>
<p>Earlier on Monday, Jean-Pierre Ergas, Alcan Executive Vice-President with responsibility for European operations called for the removal of the tariff, saying it was illogical, discriminatory and distorted trade.</p>
<p>Horovitz said that case can be presented to the European Commission and solutions can be found in a surprising manner.</p>
<p>The tariff is still there probably because the EU favours the interest of those who want to retain it rather than the interests of those who would wish to abolish it, he added.</p>
<p>--London Newsroom 44 171 542 8080</p>
